%description:

Tcp.fastopenExpOptionEnabled:off -- after a request without an answer, the next request uses kind 34 again.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "TCP Fast Open: also use the experimental option (kind 254 with the magic
number 0xF989, RFC 7413 appendix A), as Linux: the server answers it in the same option, and the
client tries it once when a cookie request had no answer".

The classes: off (false, the default) and on (true, the value of LinuxTcp), with the facets
client and server. The code: TcpConnection::processSegmentInSynSent() lets a client whose
cookie request got no answer use the experimental option at the next request, once, with true;
TcpConnection::readHeaderOptions() lets a server read the experimental option, and
TcpConnection::writeHeaderOptions() answers in it. The parameter is @mutable, but it acts only at
the SYN-ACK of a cookie request without an answer and at the next open, so the open connection
shows no old value that a later step can see: no test of a class changed can separate it. This test separates "off" from "on".

Host under test: host1, a Fast Open client (precondition); host2 has no Fast Open, so the cookie
request of connection 1 at 0.1 s gets no answer. host1 opens connection 2 at 0.5 s with Fast
Open. Observation: level 1, the SYN of connection 2 requests a cookie in the option of kind 34, and
has no experimental option.

%# parameter: Tcp.fastopenExpOptionEnabled
%# class: off
%# value: false
%# key: *.host1.tcp.fastopenExpOptionEnabled
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpMutations.h"
#include "TcpTestApp.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpFastopenExpOptionEnabledOff")
        .act(action().at(0.1).run<TcpTestApp>("host1.app[0]", [](TcpTestApp& app) { app.socket().connect(app.addressOf("host2"), 1000, true); })
                 .describe("host1 opens connection 1 with Fast Open"))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) {
                      std::string cookie = tcpFastOpenCookieOf(context.event.packet);
                      return cookie == "";
                  })
                  .describe("the SYN of connection 1: a cookie request")
                  .within(0.5))
        .act(action().at(0.5).run<TcpTestApp>("host1.app[1]", [](TcpTestApp& app) { app.socket().connect(app.addressOf("host2"), 1000, true); })
                 .describe("host1 opens connection 2 with Fast Open"))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) {
                      std::string cookie = tcpFastOpenCookieOf(context.event.packet);
                      return cookie == "" && tcpFastOpenCookieOf(context.event.packet, true) == "-";
                  })
                  .describe("the SYN of connection 2: a request of kind 34")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpFastopenExpOptionEnabledOff compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.fastopenExpOptionEnabled = false    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.fastopenClientEnabled = true
*.host1.numApps = 2
*.host1.app[*].typename = "TcpTestApp"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpFastopenExpOptionEnabledOff: PASS
